## Install

Container, from the release folder, with either engine:

```sh
podman load -i ntfy-image-@NTFY_VERSION@-amd64.tar
# or: docker load -i ntfy-image-@NTFY_VERSION@-amd64.tar
cp env.example .env
# set NTFY_BASE_URL to the public HTTPS name
podman-compose up -d
```

The image carries the stable local tag `localhost/ntfy:server`, which
`compose.yml` names. A server-only update ships a new tarball and needs the load
plus a container recreate; the relay and its config do not change.

Windows relay, from the unpacked zip:

```powershell
& .\ntfy-deploy.exe install
```

Linux relay, from the unpacked tarball:

```bash
~/.local/share/ntfy-relay/ntfy-deploy install
```

`install` places the four binaries in the relay root, writes the missing
configs, registers the startup and watchdog entries, and starts the relay. A
root without a token is not a failure: the relay waits for `ntfy-token.txt` and
attaches it when `ntfy-auth apply` writes it.

On a host without systemd, such as Alpine, the installer writes no unit and no
`systemctl` call, prints `unit: skipped, no systemd (systemctl not found)` with
a warning that no autostart and no watchdog were installed, and still starts
the relay. A host whose `systemctl` answers but whose user manager is not
running takes the same path, reports `unit: skipped, no systemd user session (systemctl --user show-environment failed)`, and still starts the relay. The
last line reads `result: installed, without autostart`. The token file is
copied from the host that runs the container, because `ntfy-auth` drives
`podman exec` there.

Then:

```sh
ntfy-auth apply
```

The relay serves `https://<your name>` through a TLS terminator on 443 to the
container's loopback port; the phone subscribes to the project folders as
topics. See `docs/specsheet/10-deploy-mode.md` and `docs/operations.md`.

## Phone

Plain text goes to the chat last typed in on the desktop. `/new <title>` creates
and targets a session, `/to` lists five, `/auto` drops the target. Permission
asks take `/allow`, `/always`, or `/deny`. A question form takes `/answer`
followed by any text: the relay closes the newest form with that text as the
reason, and the assistant reads it and asks again when it needs more.

## Checks

The release workflow built the four binaries for the three targets and the
image from the tagged tree, and ran the workspace tests. The smoke suite,
`e2e-smoke-tests/smoke.mjs`, runs eight checks against the live server. The
manual is published as HTML from the repository's `docs/` tree.
